# OpenMP / MPI Code Style

## Defaults

- Prefer C (and only C) as used in the current task/file; don't switch language without reason.
- Keep programs self-contained and readable for learning (clear variable names, short comments only where the parallel idea is non-obvious).
- Check return codes for MPI calls that can fail in student setups (`MPI_Init`, `MPI_Finalize`, sends/recvs when debugging).

## OpenMP

- Set team size via `OMP_NUM_THREADS` / CLI args when the task cares about scaling; don't hardcode thread counts unless required.
- Prefer `reduction`, `private`/`shared`, and `schedule` explicitly when correctness depends on them.
- Avoid unprotected shared writes; call out races if left as an exercise.

## MPI

- Always pair `MPI_Init` / `MPI_Finalize`.
- Prefer collective ops (`Bcast`, `Reduce`, `Scatter`/`Gather`) when they match the pattern; point-to-point only when needed.
- Rank 0 prints summaries; other ranks stay quiet unless debugging communication.

## Build / run (typical)

```bash
# OpenMP
gcc -fopenmp -O2 program.c -o program && ./program

# MPI
mpicc -O2 program.c -o program && mpirun -np 4 ./program
```

Use the project's Makefile if present.
